 <section id="observer-input"><h2>Observer's read-only sight — explicit live capture candidate, not a continuous hook</h2><p>The retired <a href="../../observer-index-lab/build_index.py">Observer index lab</a> normalized several native streams into SQLite, but its rebuild script can delete/recreate its own database and references a discarded wiki path: do not run it against live state. MemoryD's separate <a href="../memoryd-harness/ayautc/src/schema.sql">Rust archive</a> already retains exact source positions, hashes and a read-only <code>observer_source_refs</code> projection, but its worktree and recurring jobs are paused/dirty. Leave both owners untouched.</p><p>The isolated <a href="observer-probe/">Rust Observer probe</a> accepts only an explicitly selected JSONL file of candidate <a href="../schema-observer-ingest-envelope-v0.json">ingest envelopes</a>. It checks profile, native producer/instance/stream/position, bounded length and the selected optional declarations (not full JSON Schema validation); malformed declared IDs fail rather than become absent, and a present source generation must match the schema's lowercase hex format. It copies only explicit native event/session/turn/trace/span IDs to a sanitized projection. It never copies payload text, infers IDs from tool arguments, assigns UUIDv7 to every span, asserts effect success, writes Redis/SQL, reads hidden session stores or invokes a model. Missing IDs remain missing. A bounded standard-library channel with up to 16 workers returns records in source order; Crossbeam is not present in the offline cache, and Rayon would be premature for this small parse/I-O slice. The JSONL projection was tested on synthetic data; it does not run as a deployed Observer hook.</p><p><strong>Milestone A — real source capture, not a lesson:</strong> the adjacent Rust adapter explicitly opens one named Hermes profile's <code>state.db</code> read-only and selects one exact native session. It emits the existing candidate envelope with empty <code>semantics</code> and <code>payload</code>, preserving SQLite <code>messages.id</code> as <code>native_event_id</code>/<code>source_position</code>, the recorded <code>session_id</code>, and a <code>platform_message_id</code> only when actually present. It does not invent <code>turn_id</code>, <code>trace_id</code> or <code>span_id</code> from temporal proximity. <code>source_generation_id</code> is a profile-local hash of the canonical SQLite path and file device/inode; <code>record_sha256</code> hashes the selected serialized SQLite row fields, and <code>captured_at</code> records first inspection time. Identity is profile + producer + generation + native session + stream + row position: appending rows does not rename old rows. A private atomic checkpoint maps that key to its envelope; replay checks every stored key against the source snapshot (including deletion masked by one or more appends), compares each selected row and preserves the original valid first-inspection time. Malformed persisted capture fields or live payload/semantics/evidence annotations are rejected, not silently reused. Scope lookup and message reads share one read transaction, so a concurrent profile change cannot label later rows with an earlier profile snapshot. These are bounded candidate tests, not crash recovery or hostile-file authentication. Replay never re-executes tools.</p><p><strong>Local evidence is not telemetry export:</strong> an authorized profile-local resolver reopens the exact SQLite row and validates its stored digest before returning the original message/tool content to its local caller. The capture CLI prints counts only; neither payload text, content hashes, native IDs nor source paths are automatically approved for Respan/Datadog export. A hash is integrity evidence for inspected bytes, not evidence that a claim is true. One non-sensitive one-shot Hermes turn with a read-only <code>read_file</code> call was captured and replayed from a second process with no duplicate accepted source keys; this proves only the bounded test run, not ongoing coverage or crash/retention behavior. No UUIDv7 occurrence or memory ID is minted for these raw rows.</p><p><strong>Milestone B — isolated fixture proof, not Hermes transport:</strong> the explicit test driver creates a disposable regular <code>workspace/target.txt</code>, freezes exact BEFORE/EXPECTED bytes and a logical-path target in <code>intent.json</code>, launches one separate writer process with no automatic retry, then acquires fresh readback in a separate reader process on the bound original target. The passive Rust <a href="observer-probe/src/effect.rs">effect verifier</a> reads retained run evidence only; it cannot dispatch the writer or inspect the live target. A tool's success text and exit code are stored apart from the same-target postcondition/change checks. Reported success without effect and a changed decoy fail; missing, stale/other-attempt or wrong-target-only evidence and changed frozen intent stay unknown. An already satisfied target has a verified postcondition but no new transition. Restoration and its readback are separate from the historical receipt; a failed cleanup is visible without rewriting a verified effect. The <a href="schemas/effect-receipt.v0.2.candidate.schema.json">manually authored candidate receipt schema</a> uses the candidate memory verification vocabulary without minting <code>memory_id</code> or promoting memory. A local digest seal anchors the saved receipt and evidence for passive historical replay, not tamperproof same-user authentication; replay neither invokes the writer nor reads today's target. This is a controlled single-writer fixture, not general causal attribution, a production Observer hook, a repaired episode, or a native Hermes write-path test. A future real Hermes transport qualification must link the genuine native dispatch and original-target readback separately. The order is native source available → capture references/integrity → identify a source-backed bounded event/episode → propose metadata/interpretation → appropriate independent checks/review → curated memory. Capture does not wait for Decide/Jev or a lesson selector. Future OTel and external-source adapters require their own permission, privacy, retention and correlation reviews; time adjacency alone is not causal evidence.</p></section>
 <section id="source-intake"><h2>Source intake is not memory promotion — candidate design</h2><p><strong>Observer observes ingestion and use, not the entire outside world.</strong> Separate a source adapter that obtains data, staging that retains unreviewed captures, Observer records of actual system actions, and Brainstem's review/promotion decisions. A captured item is neither endorsed nor a durable memory. Every source occurrence keeps URL/native ID, producer/feed, published/fetched times when supplied, content hash, access/cleaning revision, privacy scope and source position; never call a fetch receipt an independently verified claim.</p><ul><li><strong>Event-driven runtime:</strong> instrument authorized agent/tool/OTel events as they happen, preserving native IDs, explicit correlation basis and outcome-unknown until a verifier observes the same target. Avoid duplicating one UUIDv7 occurrence per span.</li><li><strong>On-demand research:</strong> an explicitly requested Firecrawl/web fetch can be cleaned and hashed immediately; record fetch, selected section, actual use in a task, and eventual outcome separately. Retain the source snapshot in staging/cold storage if permitted; selection and use do not prove the page's claims.</li><li><strong>Scheduled external intake:</strong> RSS/news items enter a bounded, deduplicated staging queue with feed URL, published/fetched time, hash and retrieval status. The batch-run event and processed/selected items may have Observer receipts; mere appearance in a feed does not mint accepted occurrences or memories. Exact filters precede optional Decide metadata and BGE/GLiNER grouping; Brainstem reviews worthwhile clusters with an explicit backlog, false-negative sampling and privacy gate. Stronger reasoning proposes source-linked promotion only when needed.</li></ul><p>Long-term Basic Memory notes are deliberate, reviewed knowledge; Obsidian may be an optional cold human view, not a required machine store. Do not restart paused recurring jobs, assume continuous Basic Memory sync, or claim Firecrawl/RSS adapters are connected from this HTML. Retention, deletion and staged-source privacy policy need independent owner review before a live pipeline.</p></section>
 <section id="gaps"><h2>Open gates</h2><p>The 30-minute reviewer maintains a linked, source-backed <a href="docs/reviews/memory-gaps.html">HTML gap/issue register</a> and <a href="docs/reviews/memory-gaps.v0.json">bounded JSON record</a>, not a second authority for memory semantics. Findings cite current code, this guide and JSON contracts; the development lane changes primary files and the reviewer checks evidence before closing an issue. This project-scoped pattern can be adapted by future builds after their owners review it.</p><ul><li>Review this human-readable HTML vocabulary and the manually authored candidate memory schema/fixture together; validate agreement and branch-specific preconditions. No compiler is required for comprehension, and this is not a production cutover.</li><li>Decide the first imported Jev contract and exact catalog owner/location. The current Brainstem catalog is not configured.</li><li>Benchmark candidate coverage, abstention, privacy, latency and cost on frozen fixtures. A three-case Decide smoke test is only liveness/plumbing evidence.</li><li>Decide which privacy-reviewed semantic span fields may leave the current metadata-only OTel route.</li></ul></section>
+<section id="reference-first-candidate-20261009"><h2>Reference-first candidate binding — 2026-10-09</h2><p>The additive <a href="memory_v0.py">candidate Python projection</a> consumes supplied Observer SourceEvent UUIDv7 event_id and keeps ArtifactRecord custody, immutable EvidenceEvent interpretations and existing candidate save/memory identity separate. A checkpoint adapter joins actual native generation/session/row references to supplied IDs; an AYA_UTC v0.1 adapter uses existing archive_key, source generation, byte coordinates and retained bytes. Neither issues a new occurrence ID or adopts historical index-lab projection IDs. Retry/reopen preserve occurrence identity; equal bytes do not merge distinct generations. Optional turn/trace/span links and superseding interpretations remain additive. Archive resolution verifies original native/message identity and retained bytes separately from missing, changed or unresolved current file generation/prefix evidence. UTC windows are half-open eligibility filters, not inferred causal joins. Unknown native session and unsupported source records stay unresolved. Candidate association never promotes a claim. No aya_event_id, default stream_seq, global sequence, service or PostgreSQL migration is added. This is offline candidate behavior, not canonical writer authority, accepted memory or production qualification. See <a href="docs/plans/2026-10-09-reference-first-slice.md">plan and boundaries</a>, <a href="tests/test_reference_admission.py">reference tests</a> and <a href="tests/test_capture_reference_wiring.py">capture/archive tests</a>.</p></section>
+<p>Offline fixture continuation: the existing Observer probe can accept and replay a canonical UUIDv7 SourceEvent with explicit fixture writer provenance in a sealed journal. Epoch is retained in the candidate reference projection. Production writer registration and Postgres durability remain future integration work.</p>
